WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.411
Tool poisoning, shadowing, and rug pulls.

2
00:00:02.691 --> 00:00:12.312
The tool list is itself an attack surface, because the model reads tool names and descriptions as instructions and users usually see only a short summary.

3
00:00:12.592 --> 00:00:13.680
Tool poisoning.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:33.821
In April 2025, Invariant Labs showed a tool that claimed to add two numbers while its description, which the client UI did not display in full, told the model to read the user’s MCP configuration file and SSH private key and pass them in an extra parameter, without mentioning it to the user.

5
00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:41.845
Their experiments used Cursor, and they said the attack applies to any client that does not properly display and validate descriptions.

6
00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:43.021
Shadowing.

7
00:00:43.301 --> 00:00:54.949
The same research showed that a malicious server’s description can change how the model uses a different, trusted server’s tools, for instance telling it to send every email to an attacker’s address.

8
00:00:55.229 --> 00:00:57.597
The malicious tool never has to be called.

9
00:00:57.877 --> 00:01:07.648
This is possible because all connected servers’ descriptions share one model context, which is why the local-server guide says to keep your server roster small.

10
00:01:07.928 --> 00:01:08.888
Rug pulls.

11
00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:12.667
A server can change its tool definitions after you approved it.

12
00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:16.168
Install-time review does not cover what arrives later.

13
00:01:16.448 --> 00:01:30.336
Willison’s write-up of these attacks includes an Invariant demonstration in which a server swapped its definitions to hijack a WhatsApp server’s send tool, with padding that pushed the stolen data out of view in the client UI.

14
00:01:30.616 --> 00:01:32.899
The mitigations sit with clients and with you.

15
00:01:33.179 --> 00:01:46.747
Clients should show full descriptions, alert when they change, and display every invocation with its arguments; the local-server guide says to prefer clients that pin tool definitions at approval and re-prompt on change.

16
00:01:47.027 --> 00:02:00.552
You should read every tool description before relying on a server (the Inspector shows them with no model in the loop), remove servers whose descriptions contain instructions unrelated to the tool’s job, and pin versions.

17
00:02:00.832 --> 00:02:08.747
The specification adds one rule for clients: treat tool annotations as untrusted unless they come from a trusted server.

18
00:02:09.027 --> 00:02:11.608
A destructive tool can claim to be read-only.
